1
00:00:01,165 --> 00:00:03,342
Look at this structure from directly above.

2
00:00:03,787 --> 00:00:05,755
Three squares are occupied.

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,032
Can you tell which stack is tallest?

4
00:00:08,477 --> 00:00:12,856
You could guess, but the top view would look exactly the same if the heights
changed.

5
00:00:13,301 --> 00:00:19,007
A language model can therefore give a perfectly defensible answer and still miss
most of the possibilities.

6
00:00:19,452 --> 00:00:27,274
Our project asks how many different valid structures a frozen language model can
discover when it has a fixed number of attempts.

7
00:00:27,719 --> 00:00:34,164
We test that question on the voxel reconstruction task in HypoSpace, introduced
by Chen and colleagues.

8
00:00:36,092 --> 00:00:37,777
Start with a three by three grid.

9
00:00:38,222 --> 00:00:41,838
A colored square means at least one cube lies in that column.

10
00:00:42,283 --> 00:00:44,287
An empty square means zero.

11
00:00:44,732 --> 00:00:47,623
Gravity gives us a convenient description.

12
00:00:48,068 --> 00:00:52,901
Every occupied column is a solid stack, with no floating blocks.

13
00:00:53,346 --> 00:00:55,904
Read the occupied positions in row order.

14
00:00:56,349 --> 00:01:01,588
Each one can have height one, two, or three, independently of the others.

15
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:07,617
Multiplying those choices gives twenty-seven distinct structures for this
observation.

16
00:01:08,062 --> 00:01:10,227
Each has exactly the same footprint.

17
00:01:12,155 --> 00:01:17,603
Suppose four draws give the three stacks shown here, with the first answer
occurring twice.

18
00:01:18,048 --> 00:01:20,163
Every proposed structure is legal.

19
00:01:20,608 --> 00:01:22,650
Validity is four out of four.

20
00:01:23,095 --> 00:01:29,257
Uniqueness counts three distinct proposals out of four attempts, so it reaches
seventy-five percent.

21
00:01:29,702 --> 00:01:31,989
Recovery uses a different denominator.

22
00:01:32,434 --> 00:01:38,178
Three distinct valid structures cover only three of the twenty-seven possible
structures.

23
00:01:38,623 --> 00:01:41,550
This example shows why we track recovery through time.

24
00:01:41,995 --> 00:01:47,000
The second copy consumes a draw and leaves the set of discoveries unchanged.

25
00:01:48,928 --> 00:01:54,217
Imagine choosing among all twenty-seven answers uniformly and independently.

26
00:01:54,662 --> 00:02:01,279
One particular answer is missed with probability twenty-six over twenty-seven on a
single draw.

27
00:02:01,724 --> 00:02:05,499
Raise that probability to the twenty-seventh power to miss it every time.

28
00:02:05,944 --> 00:02:11,368
Subtracting from one gives the expected fraction recovered, about sixty-four
percent.

29
00:02:11,813 --> 00:02:15,281
Even this generous reference leaves many answers unseen.

30
00:02:15,726 --> 00:02:23,338
If the model concentrates probability on a few familiar patterns, independent
draws revisit those patterns even more often.

31
00:02:23,783 --> 00:02:28,814
The second speaker will show how earlier proposals can change the next draw.

32
00:02:31,193 --> 00:02:35,260
Our decoding experiment gives every method the same compact interface.

33
00:02:35,716 --> 00:02:40,084
The model sees the observation and proposes one height for each occupied cell.

34
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:49,066
For example, two, one, three represents a two-cube stack in the first cell, a single
cube in the second, and three cubes in the last.

35
00:02:49,522 --> 00:02:52,971
Each chosen digit comes from the legal height alphabet.

36
00:02:53,427 --> 00:02:59,491
Expanding these digits into layers gives a complete structure that can be scored
against the observation.

37
00:02:59,946 --> 00:03:05,553
This representation makes it possible to study repeated answers while holding
output formatting fixed.

38
00:03:07,528 --> 00:03:09,617
Consider three earlier proposals.

39
00:03:10,072 --> 00:03:12,957
Two of them ended in one after the prefix two, one.

40
00:03:13,412 --> 00:03:16,383
The third ended in three after the same prefix.

41
00:03:16,839 --> 00:03:23,433
If the next proposal starts with two, one, those counts tell us exactly which endings
have already attracted probability.

42
00:03:23,889 --> 00:03:29,046
A single count per position would mix this history together with proposals that
started differently.

43
00:03:29,502 --> 00:03:32,202
The prefix table keeps those cases separate.

44
00:03:32,657 --> 00:03:37,128
It is small enough to inspect, and it changes as the model draws more structures.

45
00:03:39,103 --> 00:03:43,677
At a decision point, we subtract a penalty from each model log probability.

46
00:03:44,132 --> 00:03:47,844
The penalty grows as the logarithm of one plus the visit count.

47
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:53,068
Its scale depends on the gap between the model's largest and smallest height
scores.

48
00:03:53,524 --> 00:03:56,866
That lets one previous visit counter a sharp local preference.

49
00:03:57,322 --> 00:04:03,640
Normalizing the adjusted scores gives a new probability distribution over the three
possible heights.

50
00:04:04,096 --> 00:04:09,797
The decoder then samples, advances one position, and eventually records the
finished proposal.

51
00:04:10,252 --> 00:04:12,885
Drawing a duplicate still spends one attempt.

52
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:17,030
Only the proposals seen for this observation enter its history.

53
00:04:17,486 --> 00:04:20,742
The strategy never needs a catalog of all the answers.

54
00:04:22,717 --> 00:04:26,558
The model-only baseline measures what the shared interface can do on its own.

55
00:04:27,014 --> 00:04:33,989
Complexity stratification builds on the paper's idea of distributing attention across
structures of different sizes.

56
00:04:34,445 --> 00:04:37,367
For stacks, size is the total number of cubes.

57
00:04:37,822 --> 00:04:41,026
A position-only count provides a closer comparison.

58
00:04:41,482 --> 00:04:47,370
It can encourage every height at every position while still overlooking
combinations between positions.

59
00:04:47,825 --> 00:04:52,448
The conditional version remembers which earlier choices led to the current
position.

60
00:04:52,903 --> 00:04:58,785
Comparing these alternatives helps identify what information in the history actually
helps.

61
00:05:00,968 --> 00:05:03,513
Our third section describes the paired experiment.

62
00:05:03,882 --> 00:05:10,974
We used Qwen two point five, three billion parameters on a server with an RTX
forty eighty Super graphics card.

63
00:05:11,343 --> 00:05:18,905
Across three difficulty levels we evaluated all one hundred twenty-nine possible
footprints with one, two, or three occupied cells.

64
00:05:19,275 --> 00:05:24,670
The hardest level uses 84 three-column layouts and 20 decoding seeds per layout
in the hardest setting.

65
00:05:25,039 --> 00:05:32,110
Every method receives the same observation, the same alphabet, and the same
budget of twenty-seven proposals in a hard-level run.

66
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,842
Each proposal corresponds to a complete set of column heights.

67
00:05:36,212 --> 00:05:40,687
When a sampled structure repeats, it remains in the trace and counts towards that
total.

68
00:05:41,057 --> 00:05:45,165
This lets us compare coverage at each draw as well as after the final draw.

69
00:05:46,766 --> 00:05:54,420
At the end of twenty-seven draws, unmodified model sampling recovered 19.7
percent of the valid structures on average.

70
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:57,927
Prefix feedback reached 79.7 percent.

71
00:05:58,296 --> 00:06:03,589
That is a paired difference of 59.9 percentage points across the same layouts and
seeds.

72
00:06:03,959 --> 00:06:11,960
The seed-panel bootstrap interval for that difference runs from 59.7 to 60.2 points,
conditional on these layouts.

73
00:06:12,330 --> 00:06:14,373
All methods produce legal vectors here.

74
00:06:14,743 --> 00:06:19,893
The change in recovery therefore comes from finding different structures within
the fixed number of attempts.

75
00:06:21,495 --> 00:06:24,244
The horizontal axis counts complete proposals.

76
00:06:24,613 --> 00:06:29,395
The vertical axis shows the mean fraction of valid structures recovered by that
point.

77
00:06:29,765 --> 00:06:37,214
After nine draws, the baseline has recovered 13.0 percent and the prefix method
has recovered 33.0 percent.

78
00:06:37,584 --> 00:06:43,337
At eighteen draws, the corresponding values are 17.1 and 61.0 percent.

79
00:06:43,706 --> 00:06:46,986
A curve starts to flatten when repeats consume more of the new draws.

80
00:06:47,356 --> 00:06:50,841
Comparing the curves at the same point keeps the query budget fixed.

81
00:06:52,442 --> 00:06:56,428
The marginal-count variant recovers 45.8 percent on this setting.

82
00:06:56,797 --> 00:07:02,346
Restricting the counts to the actual path raises recovery to 79.7 percent.

83
00:07:02,715 --> 00:07:08,438
That gap measures the value of conditioning the feedback on earlier digits, with
the model and the strength rule held fixed.

84
00:07:08,807 --> 00:07:12,220
The complexity-stratified decoder reaches 44.2 percent.

85
00:07:12,590 --> 00:07:19,119
It allocates the exact histogram of total cube counts, although layouts inside a
single size group can still repeat.

86
00:07:19,489 --> 00:07:22,217
The easiest level reverses their order.

87
00:07:22,587 --> 00:07:29,014
With one occupied cell, stratification recovers every height, while prefix feedback
recovers 87.8 percent.

88
00:07:31,157 --> 00:07:36,409
In the terminal, the inspect command selects the observation, policy, and draw
number.

89
00:07:36,851 --> 00:07:41,748
This example stops after four proposals and lists every sampled height vector in
order.

90
00:07:42,189 --> 00:07:44,337
The recovery counter is cumulative.

91
00:07:44,779 --> 00:07:47,244
A line marked new adds one structure to the set.

92
00:07:47,685 --> 00:07:50,367
A repeated vector leaves that counter where it was.

93
00:07:50,809 --> 00:07:59,817
For the selected draw, the detailed trace reports the partial prefix, the original log
probabilities, the visit counts, and the final sampling probabilities.

94
00:08:00,259 --> 00:08:04,858
That is enough information to see exactly why a previously frequent digit lost
weight.

95
00:08:06,772 --> 00:08:09,214
The browser replays the archived experiment.

96
00:08:09,656 --> 00:08:15,625
Select a footprint to fix the observation, choose a decoder, and follow one
proposal at a time.

97
00:08:16,067 --> 00:08:22,796
The stack diagram turns the numeric heights back into cubes, so you can see what
each new vector means in three dimensions.

98
00:08:23,237 --> 00:08:25,515
Compare the structures with their common footprint.

99
00:08:25,957 --> 00:08:29,559
Their appearance from above remains the same even as the heights change.

100
00:08:30,001 --> 00:08:33,420
The terminal and the presentation view use the same measured run.

101
00:08:33,862 --> 00:08:37,341
The interactive display can run locally from the saved results.

102
00:08:39,255 --> 00:08:42,270
An additional ablation changes how we interpret the gain.

103
00:08:42,712 --> 00:08:49,295
Give all three heights equal starting scores, while keeping the model's original
score spread to calibrate the visit penalty.

104
00:08:49,737 --> 00:08:58,041
Recovery rises to ninety-four point four percent, above the full prefix policy's
seventy-nine point seven percent.

105
00:08:58,483 --> 00:09:03,534
Flattening both the scores and their spread gives seventy-four point eight percent.

106
00:09:03,975 --> 00:09:08,781
On this task, calibration helps, and the model's height preferences can cost
coverage.

107
00:09:09,223 --> 00:09:13,908
Validity is built into the representation, and exact enumeration is possible.

108
00:09:14,350 --> 00:09:18,125
Whether these effects transfer to other domains remains an open question.

109
00:09:20,038 --> 00:09:22,075
The project connects three pieces.

110
00:09:22,517 --> 00:09:25,166
A frozen model supplies local preferences.

111
00:09:25,608 --> 00:09:29,662
An online memory records the choices made along each sampled path.

112
00:09:30,103 --> 00:09:34,705
A deterministic score turns the resulting sequence into a coverage curve.

113
00:09:35,147 --> 00:09:43,021
You can inspect the individual draws, reproduce the comparisons, and replay the
measurements in the browser using the public repository shown here.

114
00:09:43,463 --> 00:09:51,896
In the tested three-column setting, the prefix decoder increased mean recovery by
59.9 points over the frozen-model sampling baseline.

115
00:09:52,337 --> 00:09:57,033
The original benchmark and its authors' implementation are linked in the
repository.

116
00:09:57,474 --> 00:09:58,822
Thank you for watching.
